 Native Windows ARM64 typecheck, lint, PostgreSQL/PostGIS domain tests and browser workflow checks have been exercised using Node 24.22.2. CI separately checks Windows/Linux and standard PostgreSQL/PostGIS. Browser tests inspect public/admin/community/profile accessibility, language switching, mobile width and denied access. Browser tests also check every DESIGN_SYSTEM token and contrast pair, 44px targets, the focus ring, reduced motion, forced colors, reflow at 320px in English and Spanish, and that no page requests Google Fonts. PR check results are authoritative for each commit. No production deployment is configured.
 
 ## Identity and route contract
 
 Clerk verifies hosted account identity; application roles are always loaded from the private membership table, never user-editable metadata. A new verified user gets an account with no memberships; roles come only from approved memberships in the private membership table. The non-production admin bootstrap command can register/promote the founder's exact Clerk user ID.
 
 One account may hold several memberships (admin, organization, community), each with its own scope and approval, and only approved memberships grant privileges. Independence rule: no admin or verifier is assigned, sees in a queue or verifies a listing of an organization they are affiliated with, whether that affiliation is approved or pending. Any future organization-membership grant or claim flow must release that organization's verification work currently assigned to the new member, using the same release logic as `approveContributor`.
 
 The local launcher instead creates a random process secret and enables synthetic identities only on loopback. POST /api/dev-session requires that mode, a loopback request URL, and the exact configured Origin; it issues an HTTP-only SameSite=Strict signed one-hour session. It is rejected in production and hosted environments. No role is taken from a session token; membership is reloaded per request. GET /sign-in renders local identity choices only in that local mode, otherwise the configured Clerk sign-in or an honest configuration notice. GET /api/health is public and reports only dependency health, with 503 on failure.
 
+GET /dev/components renders the design-system component catalog only in local launcher mode: config() accepts ORAN_LOCAL_DEV=1 (development stage, loopback database), NODE_ENV is not production, VERCEL is unset and the Host is loopback; otherwise it renders the not-found page. It reads no identity, profile or catalog data, is marked noindex and is not linked from navigation.
+
 Framework agent-file generation is disabled to preserve AGENTS.md. next-env.d.ts and .next are generated/ignored; typecheck runs next typegen before TypeScript so it also works immediately after a fresh clone.
 
 ## Native onboarding and browser isolation
 
 scripts/run.ps1 prepares the exact Windows Node and pnpm pins in .local/toolchain, verifies the official Node archive SHA-256, and changes PATH only for its process. It works with the founder's existing global Node version untouched. A frozen install and helper version check were exercised on ARM64.
 
 The browser launcher uses --test: an in-memory database, fixed port 3100 and ORAN_TEST_RUN=1 selecting .next-test. It never reuses the interactive development database/server. ORAN_TEST_RUN=1 also turns off Turbopack's saved dev cache (`experimental.turbopackFileSystemCacheForDev` in next.config.ts), so every browser run compiles cold. A cache that an earlier run left can make Next 16.4 answer each HMR subscribe with "restart", which reloads the page in a loop (vercel/next.js#100251). Normal development keeps the cache, binds explicitly to port 3000 and fails if that port is occupied. Test database creation for externally supplied integration credentials uses unique disposable database names, never an existing catalog; the CI credentials need CREATEDB. Both migration idempotence and repeated integration runs are checked.
 
 ## Proposed conversational architecture (ADR-0004; not implemented)
 
